  "intro": "# Harassment tries\n\nSome opponents will not build a setup against you. They come straight at the bishop on f4 instead, or grab a square in the centre, hoping you have only memorised the quiet lines. There are three of these and each has one answer.\n\n## ...Nh5, hitting the bishop\n\nAnswer Bg3 every time. If Black takes with ...Nxg3, recapture with the h-pawn. That opens the h-file straight at the black king and your rook is already on it. You have swapped a bishop for a knight and been paid an attacking file for the trouble. Never recapture with the f-pawn: it wrecks your kingside and shuts in the rook.\n\nIf Black leaves the knight sitting on h5, ignore it and develop. A knight on the rim defends nothing.\n\n## ...Bf5, copying your idea\n\nBlack gets the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain, which is a good move. Answer Bd3, offering the trade. If Black takes, recapture with the queen: it lands on the diagonal pointing at h7. If Black avoids it with ...Bg6, the bishop ends up staring at your own bishop and doing very little, so leave it there rather than trading on your opponent's terms.\n\n## ...Ne4, the lunge\n\nA knight on e4 looks frightening and is supported by nothing. Play Nd2, attacking it. If Black trades, take back with the queen and get on with the game a pair of pieces lighter and a tempo up.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nRecapturing on g3 with the f-pawn. Retreating the bishop to d2, which throws away the whole point of the opening. Chasing pieces with pawns like g4 or f3, which weakens the king. Bringing the queen out early to defend the bishop, where it gets chased around and White loses more time than the bishop was worth.",
